{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"I've used my read budget. Let me work with what I already have:\n\nFrom the reads I've done:\n1. `repository.py` lines 0-2762: imports, including `complete_inventory` from navigation\n2. `repository.py` lines 9346-9431: `self.objective, self.scope, self.corpus = objective, objective.scope, corpus`\n3. `repository.py` lines 10738-11506: `self.messages = [PromptMessage(\"user\", objective.text)]` followed by various state fields and `self._index()`\n4. `repository.py` lines 11514-11789: `_index` method\n5. `repository.py` lines 11797-12680: `build_request` method\n6. `repository.py` lines 12688-14556: `prepare` method (first part)\n7. `repository.py` lines 16655-17325: end of prepare method\n8. `repository.py` lines 26535-29163: `apply` method\n9. `navigation.py` lines 6149-6625: `prepare_navigation` - appends task_entry when policy is not None and len(messages) == 1\n10. `navigation.py` lines 3625-3827: `task_entry` signature\n11. `navigation.py` lines 10999-12595: `complete_inventory` function\n12. `source.py` lines 990-3212: `SourceScope` class with `observe` and `check_current`\n13. `focused.py` lines 4591-5283: test assertions for metadata\n\nNow let me piece together what I know about the `__init__` method:\n\nFrom span 9346-9431: `self.objective, self.scope, self.corpus = objective, objective.scope, corpus`\nFrom span 10738-11506: \n```python\n        self.messages = [PromptMessage(\"user\", objective.text)]\n        self.disclosures: list[SourceRangeDisclosureOption] = []\n        self.source_results: list[ContextDisclosure] = []\n        self.active_disclosures: tuple[SourceRangeDisclosureOption, ...] = ()\n        self.projection_outcomes: list[ProjectionOutcome] = []\n        self.projection_error: BaseException | None = None\n        self._source_messages: dict[int, tuple[SourceRangeDisclosureOption, ...]] = {}\n        self._check_feedback: set[int] = set()\n        self.requests: list[ModelRequest] = []\n        self.responses: list[ModelResponse] = []\n        self.check_results: list[tuple[FixedCheckResult, ...]] = []\n        self.last_completion = CodingCompletion()\n        self._index()\n```\n\nSo the `__init__` sets up messages with just the objective text first. Then in `prepare`, it calls `prepare_navigation` which appends a task entry if navigation policy is set and there's only 1 message (the initial one).\n\nNow, the requirement is:\n- The first RepositoryCodingHost model request contains exactly one complete authorized resource inventory, as distinct metadata.\n- Keep the exact objective first and present.\n- No inventory-only inference step is necessary.\n- Use the accepted representation contract (`complete_inventory` in navigation.py).\n- All resources must have correct addresses, UTF-8 sizes, content identities and editable flags.\n- Repository and snapshot IDs must match current observed scope.\n- The complete inventory is not the ranked/incomplete task-candidate view. Both must coexist when task navigation is enabled.\n- Repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventory.\n- After an admitted source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\n- Inventory does not disclose source text, unauthorized paths or absolute roots; it creates no source acquisition or edit-witness credit.\n- Keep exact source bindings, currentness, retained native history and custody intact. Inserting metadata must not misalign source-message or check-feedback indices.\n- Count complete inventory in full serialized ModelRequest capacity, including Tools and other Context. If mandatory metadata cannot fit, refuse explicitly with a budget/capacity outcome before model entry; projection must not silently discard it.\n- Preserve objective, native boundaries and existing compaction.\n- Keep initial metadata and installed-source refresh behavior available without requiring optional task navigation to be enabled.\n\nSo the inventory needs to:\n1. Be added as a message in `self.messages` (distinct from the objective)\n2. Be present in the first request\n3. Not duplicate on repeated prepare calls\n4. Refresh after source changes (in `apply`)\n5. Coexist with task navigation when enabled\n6. Be counted in capacity checks\n\nLet me think about where to add it:\n\nOption A: Add it in `__init__` as a second message, and refresh it in `apply`.\n- Problem: The inventory needs the scope to be current, and it should reflect the state at prepare time. But actually, looking at the requirement \"After an admitted source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope\" - so it needs to be refreshed after apply.\n\nOption B: Add it in `prepare` before building the request, but only if not already present (to avoid duplication).\n- This is cleaner because:\n  - It naturally refreshes after source changes (since prepare is called again)\n  - We can check if it's already there to avoid duplicates\n  - It doesn't require modifying `__init__`\n\nBut wait, the requirement says \"The first RepositoryCodingHost model request contains exactly one complete authorized resource inventory.\" And \"Repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventory.\" So we need to:\n1. On first prepare: add the inventory message\n2. On subsequent prepares: update/replace it (or keep it if scope hasn't changed)\n3. After apply: the next prepare should have updated inventory\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"After an admitted source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\" This means after `apply` changes the scope, the next `prepare` call should produce a fresh inventory reflecting the new scope.\n\nThe simplest approach: In `prepare`, always regenerate the inventory message (replacing any existing one). This way:\n- First prepare: adds it\n- Subsequent prepares: replaces it with current scope data\n- After apply: the scope is updated, so next prepare generates fresh inventory\n\nBut we need to be careful about message indices. The `_source_messages` dict maps indices to disclosures, and `_check_feedback` tracks check feedback by index. If we insert a message at position 1 (after the objective), it shifts all subsequent indices.\n\nWait, let me think about this differently. The current flow is:\n1. `__init__`: `self.messages = [PromptMessage(\"user\", objective.text)]` → 1 message\n2. First `prepare`: calls `prepare_navigation` which appends task entry if policy set → 2 messages (or stays 1)\n3. Then `build_request` is called with those messages\n\nIf I add the inventory in `__init__` as a second message:\n- `self.messages = [PromptMessage(\"user\", objective.text), PromptMessage(\"user\", json.dumps(inventory))]` → 2 messages initially\n- Then `prepare_navigation` checks `len(messages) == 1` which would be False, so it wouldn't add the task entry!\n\nThat's a problem. The task navigation check is `if policy is not None and len(messages) == 1`. If I add the inventory in `__init__`, the messages list already has 2 entries, and task navigation won't fire.\n\nSo I need to either:\n1. Modify the condition in `prepare_navigation` (but that's in navigation.py which I can edit)\n2. Add the inventory after `prepare_navigation` in the prepare flow\n3. Use a different approach\n\nActually wait - let me re-read the requirement: \"The complete inventory is not the ranked/incomplete task-candidate view. Both must coexist when task navigation is enabled.\"\n\nSo both the inventory AND the task entry should be present. The current `prepare_navigation` only adds the task entry when `len(messages) == 1`. If I add the inventory before that check, it won't fire.\n\nOptions:\n- Add inventory after `prepare_navigation` in `prepare`\n- Modify `prepare_navigation` to also account for inventory\n- Add inventory in `__init__` and modify the condition\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest given the constraints:\n- repository.py has only 737 bytes of headroom\n- navigation.py has substantial space\n\nThe requirement says \"Keep initial metadata and installed-source refresh behavior available without requiring optional task navigation to be enabled.\" This means the inventory should be added regardless of whether navigation is enabled.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is:\n1. In `repository.py`'s `prepare` method, after calling `prepare_navigation`, add/refresh the inventory message\n2. In `apply`, after updating scope, remove or mark the old inventory so it gets regenerated\n\nBut wait - if I'm adding a message in `prepare`, I need to be careful about:\n- Not duplicating (check if already present)\n- Not shifting indices for `_source_messages` and `_check_feedback`\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The messages list is the history. Once a request is sent, the messages become part of the conversation. If I modify `self.messages` in `prepare`, I'm modifying the conversation history.\n\nLooking at the flow more carefully:\n- `self.messages` starts with `[PromptMessage(\"user\", objective.text)]`\n- In `prepare`, `prepare_navigation` may append to it\n- Then `build_request(tuple(self.messages))` creates the request\n- After the response, presumably messages are appended (assistant response, tool results, etc.)\n\nSo `self.messages` is the full conversation. The inventory should be a message that's present from the start and gets updated when scope changes.\n\nThe key insight: \"Repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventory.\" This means if prepare is called multiple times before a response (or after responses), we shouldn't add another inventory message each time.\n\nAnd \"After an admitted source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope\" - this means after `apply`, the inventory